# No use-case comments on repository methods

Repository methods are reusable data access. Do **not** add JSDoc or inline comments that name callers, product flows, or "when to pass X" scenarios.

That couples the repository to today's call sites and goes stale when a new caller appears.

## Do not write

```typescript
/**
 * Used by out-of-scope cleanup to resolve flags whose accounts left the
 * evaluation set.
 */
listActiveKeysBySystemId(...)

/**
 * Pass `["ACTIVE"]` when an admin dismiss must win a race (e.g. out-of-scope
 * cleanup).
 */
states?: readonly FlaggedAccountState[];
```

## Do instead

- Name the method and parameters for what they *do* (`listActiveKeysBySystemId`, `states`).
- Put caller-specific rationale at the call site only when the constraint is non-obvious — or nowhere if the code already says it.
- Keep repository comments limited to storage/query mechanics the signature cannot express (e.g. unindexed field, multi-tenant id shape).
